        "id": 1241955
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-07-10T17:13:11Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I would like to understand more about the motivation for this.  Based on what I know (and it\u0027s possible I am missing something), I disagree with the underlying premise that this change should be made at all.  This mechanism is meant to capture one, single wakeup reason for every resume or failed suspend (or potentially multiple wakeup IRQs if there are 2+ pending in HW in early resume flow).\n\nIf the kernel actually suspended successfully, we want to capture the IRQ(s) that caused it to resume.\n\nIf the kernel did _not_ suspend successfully, we want to capture the wakeup source (or off-nominal error) that blocked it.\n\nI am not aware of any reason why we would ever want this mechanism to capture both for a single event, and if we did, it would result in incorrect data being provided to the android framework.",
      "revId": "23483f2054c1f9362c5c1eac72874efa20fdd49c",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "6063c8b0_1ea0bdcf",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1723023
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-07-13T02:06:27Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The reason I submit this patch is that kernel would only log wakeup source names even if the suspended system is wakeup by an irq.\n\nConsidering the following cases: a wakeup source is activated during wakeup irq handler\n\nsuspend_enter\n  -\u003e s2idle_loop -\u003e system has suspended\n\n   (wakeup irq interrupt)\n   pm_system_irq_wakeup() -\u003e log_irq_wakeup_reason -\u003e irq handler -\u003e activate wakeup source \n\n   (start resume flow)\n    -\u003e pm_wakeup_pending\n      -\u003e log_suspend_abort_reason\n        -\u003e__log_abort_or_abnormal_wake(true, fmt, args);\n           -\u003e set suspend_abort true\n   ....\n   -\u003e print_wakeup_sources\n      -\u003e only print wakeup source due to suspend_abort is true and irq recorded in log_irq_wakeup_reason is not printed\n\nCould we check wakeup irq first and then check wakeup sources?",
      "parentUuid": "283f8ff2_afc1f316",
      "revId": "23483f2054c1f9362c5c1eac72874efa20fdd49c",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "620d82bc_5c5112d0",
        "filename": "/PATCHSET_LEVEL",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 0,
      "author": {
        "id": 1241955
      },
      "writtenOn": "2020-07-14T21:10:55Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I understand now... thank you for explaining.\n\nThe call behavior you describe isn\u0027t actually intentional; it\u0027s a bug.  It didn\u0027t happen before s2idle was refactored in 4.14, and it still doesn\u0027t happen in deep suspend.\n\nThe original wakeup reason logging code made a silent assumption that _only_ one of two things could happen:\n* a suspend was successful, and only (the equivalent of) log_irq_wakeup_reason() would be called during resume\n* a suspend failed, and only log_suspend_abort_reason() would be called.\n\nBut when s2idle was refactored out of the main suspend flow in 4.14, that assumed behavior was no longer the case.  In 4.14 and 4.19, s2idle flow will explicitly call pm_wakeup_pending even after a successful suspend, and - most unfortunately - after doing some device resume work, which makes it extremely likely that pm_wakeup_pending() will find some sort of wake source activity and report it as an abort.  It looks like s2idle has been refactored yet again in 5.4, so I don\u0027t know what the behavior will be there.\n\nSo the bug is that the wakeup reason logging code should not assume that log_irq_wakeup_reason() and log_suspend_abort_reason() will never be called in the same resume operation.  Instead, it should ensure that it captures the first _kind_ of event that gets logged (not just the first call, because we want to allow multiple calls to log_irq_wakeup_reason and log_threaded_irq_wakeup_reason).\n\nRight now, there are binary flags for suspend_abort and abnormal_wake.  They are used to ensure that only the first abort or abnormal wake gets logged because 1) we only want the first one and 2) they use the same text buffer.  If that\u0027s modified to no longer use a binary flag but instead use some enumerated values (e.g. RESUME_NONE, RESUME_IRQ, RESUME_ABORT, RESUME_ABNORMAL), then each API function can check to see whether it should do anything based on that flag.  So\n* log_suspend_abort_reason - do nothing unless current value is RESUME_NONE, otherwise set to RESUME_ABORT\n* log_abnormal_wakeup_reason - do nothing unless current value is RESUME_NONE, otherwise set to RESUME_ABNORMAL\n* log_irq_wakeup_reason and log_threaded_irq_wakeup_reason - do nothing unless current value is _either_ RESUME_NONE or RESUME_IRQ, otherwise set it to RESUME_IRQ\n\nI haven\u0027t had a chance to try that, but I think it should work as desired.",
      "parentUuid": "6063c8b0_1ea0bdcf",
      "revId": "23483f2054c1f9362c5c1eac72874efa20fdd49c",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889",
      "unresolved": true
